4e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-2.7656173188006505e-05,0.0,-0.0014968630857765675,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-8.344646857949556e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.6689286894688848e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-5.602820692729438e-06,-4.529942543740617e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,7983,9271,279,57668,315,264,3654,9210,11,458,27211,33323,14227,4641,311,5293,429,806,29952,572,537,304,279,4396,20314,3856,13,1260,572,17605,311,633,279,1290,4226,13,576,1378,3245,6785,1931,2750,315,400,87,3,369,892,279,57668,315,400,87,3,12348,374,279,1852,438,279,57668,315,400,87,3,50784,525,57960,37018,90,76,59,2493,15170,77,30529,2493,31716,323,57960,37018,90,79,59,2493,15170,80,41715,2493,31716,11,1380,400,76,54876,400,77,54876,400,79,54876,323,400,80,3,525,6785,25780,13,7379,400,76,38334,70001,10,80,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nWhile finding the sine of a certain angle, an absent-minded professor failed to notice that his calculator was not in the correct angular mode. He was lucky to get the right answer. The two least positive real values of $x$ for which the sine of $x$ degrees is the same as the sine of $x$ radians are $\\frac{m\\pi}{n-\\pi}$ and $\\frac{p\\pi}{q+\\pi}$, where $m$, $n$, $p$, and $q$ are positive integers. Find $m+n+p+q$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b7d9354607e23a4851e3c45e61b9bd51f990859e809902b1c839792420a00869"},"seconds":67.93312215805054,"seed":226206782041601550,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\( x \\) such that \\(\\sin(x^\\circ) = \\sin(x \\text{ rad})\\). We start by using the fact that the sine function has a periodicity and symmetry. Specifically, \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\pi - \\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we need to convert \\( x \\) degrees to radians. Recall that \\( x^\\circ = \\frac{\\pi x}{180} \\text{ rad} \\). Therefore, the equation \\(\\sin(x^\\circ) = \\sin(x \\text{ rad})\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{180}\\right) = \\sin(x)\n\\]\n\nWe use the property of the sine function that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\pi - \\theta)\\). This gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = x\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = \\pi - x\\)\n\n### Case 1: \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = x\\)\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\pi x = 180x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\pi = 180\n\\]\nThis is not possible since \\(\\pi \\neq 180\\). Therefore, there are no solutions in this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = \\pi - x\\)\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi x}{180} + x = \\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{180} + 1\\right) = \\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left(\\frac{\\pi + 180}{180}\\right) = \\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180\\pi}{\\pi + 180}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this expression:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the two least positive real values of \\( x \\). The first value is:\n\\[\nx_1 = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\n\nTo find the second value, we consider the periodicity and symmetry of the sine function. The sine function is periodic with period \\(2\\pi\\), so we also have:\n\\[\n\\sin(x) = \\sin(x + 2k\\pi) \\quad \\text{for integer } k\n\\]\n\nFor the second value, we consider:\n\\[\n\\sin(x) = \\sin(\\pi - x)\n\\]\n\nThus, the second value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[\nx_2 = \\pi - \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying \\( x_2 \\):\n\\[\nx_2 = \\pi \\left(1 - \\frac{180}{180 + \\pi}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nx_2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{180 + \\pi - 180}{180 + \\pi}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nx_2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{180 + \\pi}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nx_2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\n\nThus, the two least positive real values of \\( x \\) are:\n\\[\nx_1 = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that these values are in the form \\(\\frac{m\\pi}{n-\\pi}\\) and \\(\\frac{p\\pi}{q+\\pi}\\). Matching these forms:\n\\[\nx_1 = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 - (-\\pi)} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = 180, \\quad n = 180\n\\]\n\\[\nx_2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{\\pi + 180} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad p = 1, \\quad q = 180\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum \\( m + n + p + q \\) is:\n\\[\nm + n + p + q = 180 + 180 + 1 + 180 = 541\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{541}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{541}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
